1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
What happens when an AI agent treats a document as a command?
Synktastic lets you watch the mistake, then stop it.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Across ten OWASP lessons, you learn by testing attacks
and choosing the controls that actually hold.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Here, a model card claims release approval.
It is attacker-controlled text,

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
but the Builder might pass it along as authority.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
First, watch the unprotected route.
Builder repeats the claim. Reviewer accepts it.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Publisher reaches the simulated registry.
That is the failure we need to prevent.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Now I place one guard at Builder.
It checks where the approval came from.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Verdict blocks the document's authority claim.
Reviewer checks real evidence,

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,000
and the safe draft finishes without publishing.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
You can pause, step through each decision, reset,

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
or move the guard to Reviewer
to compare where the attack stops.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The next challenge makes you patch a different injection path.
A stronger prompt is a decoy;

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
code-checked evidence and human approval are controls.
Other modules cover data leaks and excessive agency.

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Synktastic: make the AI go rogue,
then learn how to stop it.
